*
!private/
!public/
!.gitignore

# The audit tooling lives in scratch/ and the repository's own instructions invoke it by path — AGENTS.md
# tells anyone doing a batch to run storage/app/scratch/sweep-docx-citations.php, and the docx editing
# procedure tells them to require docx-edit-engine.php. Those paths resolved to nothing on a fresh clone,
# because the `*` above ignored the whole folder, so the documented gates were only ever runnable on the one
# machine that had built them. Track the code; leave the artifacts out.
#
# The order is load-bearing: git cannot re-include a file whose directory is excluded, so scratch/ comes back
# first, its contents go away again, and only then are the code extensions let back in. That keeps out the
# 126 docx pass-backups, the .sql dumps, audit.sqlite, the prune manifests and the run logs — machine state
# and institutional data, not tools, and this repository's remote is public.
!scratch/
scratch/*
!scratch/*.php
!scratch/*.ps1
!scratch/*.mjs
# A HEAD export is a second copy of an already-tracked file, taken with `git show HEAD:<path> > scratch/laneNN-head-<name>.php`
# so a harness can run the pre-fix bytes beside the working ones and prove that *this batch* changed the verdict. Reproducible
# from the repository by definition, so tracking it would put a whole second copy of a controller — or of the 174 KB demo
# pipeline — on the remote under a name that says "tool". Each harness prints the export command and refuses (exit 1) when its
# copy is missing or byte-identical to the working file, so an ignored export can never read as a green run. The one literal
# below is lane 54's export written before the `laneNN-head-` convention existed. Consequence for whoever names a new file
# here: a real *tool* must not be named `lane*-head-*`, or this rule hides it.
scratch/lane*-head-*.php
scratch/lane54-pipeline-no-year-widening.php
# One data file is tracked, deliberately: the record of which queue rows a human has already opened is the point
# of that instrument, and it is worth nothing if it lives on only the machine that made it. It holds file paths,
# line ranges, a sentence hash and a reason per row — no institutional data, nothing a public remote must not see.
!scratch/lane70-queue-reads.json
